\apply takes the rhythm or the notes of the line of a bar
and applies notes or durations to it. It is used in a
melodic line, inside a bar definition. Its first argument
is a bar, with an instrument after a dot, or without it for
the line of the same instrument. Its syntax is the
following:

\inst1 \apply {\1.\inst2 c' <c' ees'>}

The bar can also be written before the curly brackets:

\inst1 \apply \1.\inst2 {c' <c' ees'>}

With notes, as above, the notes go to the onsets of the
line of \inst2 in bar 1. The onsets are its notes, not its
rests or notes tied from the previous note. The notes are
spread over the onsets as evenly as possible in time, each
on the onset closest to an equal part of the bar.
A note lasts until the next note or rest of the line, the
rests of the line stay as they are, and an onset without a
note right after a rest is a rest. If the line is
c'4 cis'8 r c'16 ees' r ees' cis'4
the line above gives
c'4. r8 <c' ees'>8 r4.
Every note gets the dynamic and the articulations of its
onset, unless it has its own, like below:

\inst1 \apply {\1.\inst2 c'\f-> <c' ees'>\p}

There can't be more notes than onsets.

With durations, the notes of the onsets of the line go to
the durations in turn, and start again from the first note
if there are more durations than notes:

\inst1 \apply {\1.\inst2 8 8 4 4. 8}

The durations can have dynamics and articulations
(e.g. 8\f), and a rest (e.g. r8) takes no note.

A rhythm staff can apply durations only, and they are
written as they are. A melodic line can apply notes to the
rhythm of a rhythm staff, but it can't apply the notes of a
rhythm staff to durations, as a rhythm staff has no notes.
A bar can't apply its own lines, and the durations of a
line with tuplets can't be applied to notes.
